ed) so that the required items become unresolved.\n\nThe domain: items are in order. For each item, it is unresolved if either it is failed directly or its predecessor is unresolved. So unresolved propagates forward. So if we fail an item, all items after it become unresolved. Also, if an item is already unresolved (from unresolved_without), then all items after it become unresolved. So the effect is that unresolvedness flows from left to right.\n\nGoal: find a set S of items to fail (add to unresolved set) such that all required_failure_items are in the unresolved set, given that unresolved_without items are already unresolved. We want the minimal size set S? Or minimal number of failures? Minimal cardinality? The problem statement: \"solve(case)\" – likely return the minimal set of items to fail. In the fixture, all cases are simple. So we need to implement algorithm: given list items in order (presumably index order), and two subsets: required_failure_items and unresolved_without (already unresolved). Find minimal set of items to fail such that all required items become unresolved.\n\nWe can think: if any required item is already unresolved because of an earlier item (including unresolved_without), then no need to fail that item. But if there is a required item that is not yet unresolved, we need to cause it to become unresolved. The only way is to fail some item that is at or before that required item (since failure propagates forward). But failing an item after the required item does not affect it. So we need to select a set of items to fail that covers all required items. However, due to propagation, failing an item at index i will make all items with index >= i unresolved. So we can cover multiple required items with one failure if they are all after that failure point.\n\nSo the problem reduces: given a set of required indices R (from required_failure_items), and a set of already unresolved indices U (from unresolved_without), we need to find the minimal set