rce code as a string, properly escaped. The function solve(case) must be defined.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case[\"items\"]\n    required = case[\"required_failure_items\"]\n    unresolved_without = case[\"unresolved_without\"]\n    # Map item to index\n    index_of = {item: i for i, item in enumerate(items)}\n    # Existing unresolved indices\n    existing = set()\n    for item in unresolved_without:\n        if item in index_of:\n            existing.add(index_of[item])\n    # Find min existing index, or large number\n    cov = min(existing) if existing else float('inf')\n    # Sort required by index\n    sorted_req = sorted(required, key=lambda x: index_of[x])\n    failures = []\n    for item in sorted_req:\n        idx = index_of[item]\n        if idx < cov:  # not covered\n            failures.append(item)\n            cov = idx  # new coverage\n    return failures\n```\n\nBut note: If we fail at an item, that item becomes unresolved, so coverage index becomes that item's index. However, if there is an existing unresolved item with a smaller index, that still covers. So we update cov only when we add a failure. So the condition is: if idx < cov, then it's not covered. Because if cov is the smallest index among unresolved items (existing or from previous failures), then any item with index >= cov is covered. So we compare idx < cov. Actually, if cov is the smallest unresolved index, then any item with index >= cov is covered. So not covered if idx < cov. That matches.\n\nBut careful: When we add a failure at idx, the new smallest unresolved index could be min(existing_min, idx). Since existing_min might be larger (if no existing), then cov becomes idx. But if existing_min is smaller (e.g., 0), then cov remains 0. So we should update cov = min(cov, idx). That's correct.\n\nAlternatively, we can maintain a variable `earliest_failure`? The greedy algorithm: keep the leftmost unresolved index so far. Start with min existing. For each required item in order, if its index is less than leftmost unresolved, then we must fail it (or something before it). To minimize failures, fail exactly at its index. Then set leftmost unresolved to that index. Continue. That yields failures set.\n\nImplementation: \n- leftmost = min(existing) if existing else None. If leftmost is None, then treat as not covered. We can use a sentinel like items length.\n- Iterate sorted required. For item idx, if leftmost is None or idx < leftmost, then add to failures, and set leftmost = idx.\n- Return failures.\n\nWorks for case2: existing = {0}, leftmost=0. Req idx 4: 4 < 0? False -> skip. Req idx 7: skip. No failures.\n\nCase1: existing empty, leftmost=None. Req idx0: leftmost is None -> fail, set leftmost=0. Req idx7: 7<0? false -> skip. Return [d0].\n\nCase0: existing empty, leftmost=None. Req idx1: fail, leftmost=1. Req idx4: 4<1? false -> skip. Return [d1].\n\nSo consistent.\n\nNow, ensure robustness: if required item index not in items? Probably not.\n\nThus final code.\n\nNow, we need to produce JSON with \"implementation\" key and value as string of the Python source. Escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nWrite the source code as a single line or multiline? JSON string can contain newlines as \\n. We'll keep it as a multiline string for readability, but must escape properly. In JSON, newline is escaped as \\n, backslash as \\\\, quote as \\\". We'll write the Python source with proper indentation and convert to a JSON string manually.\n\nI will write the source code:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case[\"items\"]\n    required = case[\"required_failure_items\"]\n    unresolved_without = case[\"unresolved_without\"]\n    index_of = {item: i for i, item in enumerate(items)}\n    existing = {index_of[item] for item in unresolved_without if item in index_of}\n    leftmost = min(existing) if existing else None\n    failures = []\n    for item in sorted(required, key=lambda x: index_of[x]):\n        idx = index_of[item]\